# Pre-deployment hardening (2026-08-10) — the value below
# (shop/shop_dev_password@localhost:5433) is a LOCAL-ONLY default matching
# docker-compose.yml, never something to reuse in production:
#   - Use a strong, unique, generated password for the production database
#     user — never this literal value, which is public (committed in this
#     template file).
#   - Require TLS on the connection: append `?sslmode=require` (or your
#     provider's equivalent — e.g. `?ssl=true`) to the production
#     DATABASE_URL. Most managed Postgres providers (RDS, Neon, Supabase,
#     etc.) document the exact query parameter they expect.
#   - Never point this at a database port reachable from the public
#     internet — see docker-compose.yml's own comment on why its Postgres
#     port is bound to 127.0.0.1 only.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://shop:shop_dev_password@localhost:5433/shop?schema=public"

# Same value as DATABASE_URL locally (no pooler in front of docker-compose
# Postgres). On Supabase, this must be the DIRECT connection string (port
# 5432) while DATABASE_URL is the pooled one (port 6543, `pgbouncer=true`)
# — see prisma/schema.prisma's datasource comment for why both exist.
DIRECT_URL="postgresql://shop:shop_dev_password@localhost:5433/shop?schema=public"

# Local-only Docker Compose Postgres credentials (docker-compose.yml
# reads these — never hardcoded there). Must match the user/password/db
# name embedded in DATABASE_URL above. These are throwaway local-dev
# defaults, not a real secret — still never commit a real value to any
# tracked file; this one included.
POSTGRES_USER=shop
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=shop_dev_password
POSTGRES_DB=shop

# Fulfillment/delivery config (Phase 2, extended Phase 3.3 Part 2) — see
# src/lib/fulfillment-config.ts. No admin settings UI exists yet, so these
# are the only knobs available.
PICKUP_ENABLED=true
DELIVERY_ENABLED=true
# ₹50 — current agreed Klasiq delivery fee, charged only beyond the free
# radius AND under the free-delivery subtotal threshold below.
DELIVERY_FEE_IN_PAISE=5000
# ₹1,500 — subtotal at/above which delivery is always free.
FREE_DELIVERY_THRESHOLD_IN_PAISE=150000
# 3km — route distance at/under which delivery is always free, regardless
# of subtotal. Route distance, not straight-line — see src/server/geoapify.ts.
FREE_DELIVERY_RADIUS_METERS=3000
DELIVERY_SERVICEABLE_AREA_NOTE="We currently deliver within a few kilometres of the store. If you're unsure we cover your area, choose Store Pickup or call us."

# Shop origin coordinates (Phase 3.3 Part 2) — the authoritative delivery-
# distance origin for this single-store deployment. Source: Muskan General
# Store, https://maps.app.goo.gl/rXQgWhNoQceUy3ch6
SHOP_LATITUDE=26.0329454
SHOP_LONGITUDE=83.1494211

# Geoapify (Phase 3.3 Part 2) — address autocomplete + route-distance
# calculation. See src/server/geoapify.ts (server-only; the key never
# reaches the browser). Get a key at https://www.geoapify.com/ — this file
# is the template, never put a real key here.
GEOAPIFY_API_KEY=

# Public base URL (Phase 3) — see src/lib/site-config.ts. The production
# domain is not finalized yet; leave unset for local development (falls
# back to http://localhost:3000). Set this in production so metadata,
# sitemap.xml, robots.txt and (Phase 4) QR codes point at the real domain.
# SITE_URL="https://your-production-domain-here"

# Customer Portal OTP policy (Phase 3.4 Part 1) — see src/lib/otp-config.ts.
# All optional; sane defaults apply if unset.
# OTP_EXPIRY_MINUTES=10
# OTP_MAX_ATTEMPTS=5
# OTP_RESEND_COOLDOWN_SECONDS=45
# OTP_MAX_REQUESTS_PER_WINDOW=5
# OTP_REQUEST_WINDOW_MINUTES=60
# CUSTOMER_SESSION_DURATION_DAYS=30

# WhatsApp OTP delivery (Phase 3.6 Part 1) — see
# src/server/otp/whatsapp-provider.ts and src/server/otp/provider.ts.
# REQUIRED in production: NODE_ENV=production always selects this
# provider and refuses to start (fails closed) if these are missing —
# it never silently falls back to the console dev provider. Never commit
# real values here; this file is the template, not secrets.
#
# Permanent access token for a Meta System User with
# whatsapp_business_messaging permission on your WhatsApp Business
# Account. Get one via Meta Business Settings > System Users.
WHATSAPP_API_TOKEN=
# The "Phone number ID" (NOT the phone number itself) from Meta's
# WhatsApp > API Setup page for your sending number. In production this
# ID represents Klasiq's real business number, 8542843482 — that number
# is documentation/reference only; the application never reads or
# hardcodes it anywhere, only the opaque ID Meta issues for it. There is
# no separate "Business Account ID" env var because nothing in this
# codebase calls a Meta Business-Manager-level API (template creation/
# listing, etc.) — only the per-number Cloud API message-send endpoint,
# which needs just the token and this phone number ID. If a future phase
# adds such a call, its Business Account ID must be read from its own
# new env var here, never hardcoded, following this same pattern.
WHATSAPP_PHONE_NUMBER_ID=
# The name of ONE dedicated, Meta-approved Authentication-category
# template used only for OTP delivery — never reused for order/return/
# exchange notifications (those get their own templates in a later
# phase). Must have exactly one body variable ({{1}}, the code).
WHATSAPP_OTP_TEMPLATE_NAME=
# Optional — defaults to en_US if unset. Must match the template's own
# approved language exactly.
# WHATSAPP_OTP_TEMPLATE_LANGUAGE=en_US
# Optional — Graph API version, defaults to v21.0 if unset.
# WHATSAPP_API_VERSION=v21.0

# Optional explicit provider override for NON-production environments
# only (e.g. testing real WhatsApp delivery from a staging deploy without
# setting NODE_ENV=production). Values: "console" (default) or
# "whatsapp". Has NO effect in production, where WhatsApp is always used
# regardless of this value — see getOtpProvider()'s own doc comment.
# OTP_PROVIDER=console

# WhatsApp order-lifecycle notifications (Phase 3.6 Part 2) — see
# src/server/whatsapp/notification-service.ts. Shares the SAME
# WHATSAPP_API_TOKEN/WHATSAPP_PHONE_NUMBER_ID/WHATSAPP_API_VERSION above
# (one Meta client, src/server/whatsapp/client.ts) but each event below
# has its OWN dedicated, Meta-approved template — never the OTP template,
# never shared between events. Any event whose template name is unset is
# simply skipped (logged, never a crash) — commerce never depends on
# messaging. Each template needs exactly 4 body variables, in order:
# customer name, order number, a short status line, the tracking link.
WHATSAPP_ORDER_PLACED_TEMPLATE_NAME=
WHATSAPP_ORDER_CONFIRMED_TEMPLATE_NAME=
WHATSAPP_ORDER_PREPARING_TEMPLATE_NAME=
WHATSAPP_ORDER_READY_TEMPLATE_NAME=
WHATSAPP_ORDER_DELIVERED_TEMPLATE_NAME=
# Optional — defaults to en_US if unset.
# WHATSAPP_NOTIFICATION_TEMPLATE_LANGUAGE=en_US
# Optional explicit provider override for NON-production environments
# only — same shape and same "no effect in production" guarantee as
# OTP_PROVIDER above, but its OWN, independent variable: enabling real
# WhatsApp notification sending in a staging environment never also
# enables real OTP sending there, or vice versa.
# NOTIFICATION_PROVIDER=console

# WhatsApp Return & Exchange notifications (Phase 3.6 Part 3) — see
# src/server/whatsapp/return-notification-service.ts. Shares the SAME
# transport credentials above and the SAME NOTIFICATION_PROVIDER toggle
# above (one non-OTP notification channel, not a second engine) — never
# a separate RETURN_NOTIFICATION_PROVIDER variable. Each event below has
# its OWN dedicated template, distinct from every OTP and order template.
# Any event whose template name is unset is simply skipped (logged, never
# a crash). Each template needs exactly 5 body variables, in order:
# customer name, return number, order number, a short status line, the
# tracking link. "Exchange Ready" has no template of its own — it's
# folded into WHATSAPP_EXCHANGE_COMPLETED_TEMPLATE_NAME's own
# fulfillment-aware wording (see return-notification-events.ts).
WHATSAPP_RETURN_REQUESTED_TEMPLATE_NAME=
WHATSAPP_RETURN_APPROVED_TEMPLATE_NAME=
WHATSAPP_EXCHANGE_APPROVED_TEMPLATE_NAME=
WHATSAPP_RETURN_REJECTED_TEMPLATE_NAME=
WHATSAPP_RETURN_ITEM_RECEIVED_TEMPLATE_NAME=
WHATSAPP_RETURN_COMPLETED_TEMPLATE_NAME=
WHATSAPP_EXCHANGE_COMPLETED_TEMPLATE_NAME=

# WhatsApp Invoice delivery (Phase 3.6.6 Part 3) — see
# src/server/whatsapp/invoice-notification-service.ts. Shares the SAME
# transport credentials, the SAME NOTIFICATION_PROVIDER toggle, and the
# SAME WHATSAPP_NOTIFICATION_TEMPLATE_LANGUAGE above — not a fourth
# notification channel, not a fourth language variable. Unlike every
# other event above, this one is sent on demand (an admin clicks "Send
# via WhatsApp" on the Counter Sale Success screen, Admin Order Detail,
# or the Admin Invoice page), never automatically. The template needs a
# DOCUMENT header component (the invoice PDF, attached via Meta's Media
# API — src/server/whatsapp/client.ts's uploadWhatsAppMedia) plus exactly
# 4 body variables, in order: customer name, invoice/order number, Grand
# Total, and a payment status + thank-you line (mentions an outstanding
# balance only when one exists — see buildInvoicePaymentLine). If unset,
# sending is skipped with a clear, admin-visible error — never a crash.
WHATSAPP_INVOICE_TEMPLATE_NAME=

# One-time admin bootstrap (Phase 3) — used only by `npm run db:create-admin`.
# Not read by the app itself; safe to unset after creating the first admin.
# Do NOT commit real values here — this file is the template, not secrets.
ADMIN_BOOTSTRAP_NAME="Shop Owner"
ADMIN_BOOTSTRAP_EMAIL="owner@example.com"
ADMIN_BOOTSTRAP_PASSWORD="change-this-before-running"
